<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.5.3: Choosing an Effective Strategy to Solve Quadratic Equations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02154</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.5.3: Choosing an Effective Strategy to Solve Quadratic Equations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>8d772202-c19b-4cb6-b048-ddcc941c8468</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (15 minutes)</title>
<para>This activity gives students an opportunity to practice solving quadratic equations and deciding on an effective
  strategy. Some equations can be easily solved by reasoning. Others would require solving by graphing because students
  have not yet learned the strategies to solve algebraically. Students who use graphing technology only when needed
  practice choosing tools strategically.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Give students continued access to technology.</para>
<note class="extra-support">
  <title>Support for Students with Disabilities</title><para>Engagement: Provide Access by Recruiting Interest</para>
  <para>. Leverage choice around perceived challenge. Invite students to select four of the six equations to solve. Chunking this task into more manageable parts may also support students who benefit from additional processing time.
    </para>
    <para><emphasis effect="italics">Supports accessibility for: Organization; Attention; Social-emotional skills</emphasis></para></note>

<title>Student Activity</title>
<para> Solve each equation. Be prepared to explain or show your reasoning.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(x^2=121\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(x=11\) and \(x=-11\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(x^2−31=5\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(x=6\) and \(x=-6\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>\((x−4)(x−4)=0\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\(x=4\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>\((x+3)(x−1)=5\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(x=-4\) and \(x=2\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>\((x+1)^2=-4\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> No solution</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>\((x−4)(x−1)=990\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(x=34\) and \(x=-29\)</para>
<title>Video: Choosing an Effective Strategy to Solve Quadratic Equations</title>
<para>Watch the following video to learn more about choosing an effective strategy to solve quadratic equations.</para>
<media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text"><iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/84d199f48e88269b0d50c3dc76afa178bc452da3"/></media><title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Select students to share their solutions and strategies. If not mentioned by students' explanations, highlight that:
</para>
<list>
  <item> The first three equations, as well as the equation \((x+1)^2=-4\), can be solved by reasoning, and graphing is
    not necessary. </item>
  <item> The last three equations can be solved by graphing. There are two ways to do so, as shown in a previous activity.
  </item>
  <list>
    <item> One way is to graph each side of the equation separately: \(y\) = <emphasis>expression</emphasis> on one side and \(y\) =
      <emphasis>number</emphasis> on the other side.
    </item>
    <item> Another way is to rearrange the equation such that it is in the form of <emphasis>expression</emphasis> \(= 0\), graph
      \(y\) = <emphasis>expression</emphasis>, and then find the \(x\)-intercepts. </item>
  </list>
  <item> The equation \((x+1)^2=-4\) states that some number squared is –4. Because no number can be squared to get
    a negative number, we can reason that there are no solutions. If this equation is solved by graphing
    \(y=(x+1)^2+4\), the graph would show no \(x\)-intercepts. This also tells us that there are no solutions. </item>
</list>
<title>8.5.3: Self Check</title>
<para><emphasis>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their understanding of the
    concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis></para>
<para>QUESTION:</para>
<para>Solve the equation using any method covered in the lesson.</para>
<para>\((x−6)(x+5)=-30.25\)</para>
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>Answers</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Feedback</emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        No solutions
      </entry>
      <entry>
        Incorrect. Let's try again a different way: This equation is best solved by graphing. Graph the equation
        \(y=(x−6)(x+5)+30.25\). There is one \(x\)-intercept at \((0.5,0)\). The answer is \(x=0.5\).
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(x=0.5\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        That's correct! Check yourself: This equation is best solved by graphing. Substitute 0.5 in for \(x\) and
        check to see if the equation is true. \((0.5−6)(0.5+5)=-30.25\). Since the equation is true, 0.5 is
        correct.
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(x=0.5\) and \(x=1.5\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        Incorrect. Let's try again a different way: This equation is best solved by graphing. Graph the equation
        \(y=(x−6)(x+5)+30.25\). There is one \(x\)-intercept at \((0.5,0)\). The answer is \(x=0.5\).
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(x=1.5\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        Incorrect. Let's try again a different way: This equation is best solved by graphing. Graph the equation
        \(y=(x−6)(x+5)+30.25\). There is one \(x\)-intercept at \((0.5,0)\). The answer is \(x=0.5\).
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<title>8.5.3: Additional Resources</title>
<para><emphasis>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on their experience with
    the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish to share it with
    those who could benefit from it.</emphasis></para>
<title>Choosing an Effective Strategy to Solve Quadratic Equations</title>
<para>You have learned various strategies to solve quadratic equations in different forms. If possible, try to use
  reasoning before graphing an equation.</para>
<para>Let's look at some examples.</para>
<para>Using Reasoning and the Square Root Property</para>
<para>Equations such as these can be solved by reasoning and using the square root property.</para>
<para><emphasis>Example 1</emphasis></para>
<para>  \(x^2=81\) 
  \(x=9\) and \(x=-9\) </para>

<para><emphasis>Example 2</emphasis></para>
<para>  \(x^2+7=56\)
  \(x^2=49\)
  \(x=7\) and \(x=-7\)</para>

<para><emphasis>Example 3</emphasis></para>
<para>  \((x+1)^2=9\)
  \(x=2\) and \(x=-4\)</para>
<para>Each equation above is different in some way, but they all can be solved using the same process.</para>
<para>Using Reasoning and the Zero Product Property</para>
<para>Equations such as these can be solved by reasoning and using the zero product property.</para>
<para><emphasis>Example 1</emphasis></para>
<para>   \((x−4)(x+5)=0\)
  \(x=4\) and \(x=-5\)</para>

<para><emphasis>Example 2</emphasis></para>
<para>  \((x+3)(2x+7)=0\)
  \(x=-3\) and \(x=-\frac{7}{2}\)</para>
<para>Using Graphing to Solve</para>
<para>Some equations might require graphing to be solved. One method is to rearrange the equation such that it is in the
  form of <emphasis>expression</emphasis> \(= 0\). Then graph \(y\) = <emphasis>expression</emphasis>, and find the \(x\)-intercepts to identify
  the solutions.</para>
<para>Given: \((x+2)(x−1)=4\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis>  Rewrite the equation to set it equal to 0.</para>
<para>  \((x+2)(x−1)−4=0\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis>   Graph the equation with \(y\) = the expression that equals zero.</para>
<para>  Graph \(y=(x+2)(x−1)−4\).</para>
<para><figure><media alt="GRAPH OF A PARABOLA THAT OPENS UP AND PASSES THROUGH THE POINTS (NEGATIVE 3, 0) AND (2, 0)."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/90d204f34d4adb7110ea356ebca909d5638c2b25"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> 
  Identify the \(x\)-intercepts from the graph.</para>
<para>  This graph has two \(x\)-intercepts at \((-3,0)\) and \((2,0)\).</para>
<para>The original equation \((x+2)(x−1)=4\) has two solutions at \(x=-3\) and \(x=2\).</para>

<title>Try It: Choosing an Effective Strategy to Solve Quadratic Equations</title>

<para>Solve each equation using any method.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> \((x+1)^2+6=22\) </item>
</list>
<para>Write down your answers, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<title>Solution</title>
<para>Here is how to choose an effective strategy and use it to solve quadratic equations:</para>
<para>\((x+1)^2+6=22\) 
\((x+1)^2=16\)
\(x=-5\) and \(x=3\)</para>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> \((x+3)(x−8)=0\) </item>
</list>
<para>Write down your answers, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<title>Solution</title>
<para>Here is how to choose an effective strategy and use it to solve quadratic equations:</para>
<para>\((x+3)(x−8)=0\) 
  \(x=-3\) and \(x=8\)</para>
</section>
</content>
